<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Belagavi</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Belgaum</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Belagavi, known as Belgaum until 2014, is a city in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, near the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goa">Goa</a>. Its fort dates from the 13th century, and the town was held in turn by the Bahmani and Adil Shahi sultans, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Marathas</a> and the British. The city and its district have a large Marathi-speaking population, and their status has been disputed between Karnataka and Maharashtra since 1956.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/belagavi.jpg" alt="Belagavi" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Shubham S. Sankannavar · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kamal_Basti_view,_Belgaum.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Belagavi lies in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a>, near the borders with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goa">Goa</a>, near the foothills of the Sahyadri range of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-ghats">Western Ghats</a>. It stands at an average elevation of 770 metres, about 100 kilometres from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabian-sea">Arabian Sea</a>, with the Markandeya river flowing nearby. It is 502 kilometres from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengaluru">Bangalore</a> and 500 kilometres from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Mumbai</a>, and is the administrative headquarters of the Belagavi division and district.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The town was founded in the late 12th century by the Ratta dynasty, who moved from nearby Saundatti. A Ratta official named Bichiraja built the Jain temple of Kamal Basadi, dedicated to Neminatha, in 1204. Its original name was Venugram, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> name meaning village of bamboo. In the early 13th century it passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yadava">Yadava</a> kingdom, as an inscription of 1261 attests. The Khalji dynasty of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a> invaded the region in the 14th century, after which Belgaum came under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vijayanagara-empire">Vijayanagara Empire</a>.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahmani-sultanate">Bahmani Sultanate</a> conquered it in 1474 with an army led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-gawan">Mahmud Gawan</a>. After the Bahmani state split in 1518 the town became part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adil-shahi-dynasty">Bijapur Sultanate</a>. In 1686 the Mughal emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a> overthrew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bijapur">Bijapur</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a> called the town Azamnagar. Mughal control collapsed after Aurangzeb's death in 1707, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> confederacy took the area under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peshwa">Peshwas</a>. Hyder Ali and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tipu-sultan">Tipu Sultan</a> overran it in 1776, but the Peshwa regained it after Tipu was defeated. The British annexed Belgaum in 1818. In 1824 Kitturu Chennamma, queen of Kittur, led an unsuccessful armed rebellion against them.</p><p>Belgaum hosted the 39th session of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-national-congress">Indian National Congress</a> in December 1924 under the presidency of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Mahatma Gandhi</a>. It was a major British military installation, partly because of its proximity to Portuguese Goa, and in 1961 the Indian government used forces from Belgaum to end Portuguese rule in Goa. In 1956 the States Reorganisation Act transferred Belgaum and ten taluks of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bombay-state">Bombay State</a> to Mysore State, which was renamed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a> in 1973. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a> claims the city on linguistic grounds, a dispute taken to the Supreme Court. The Maharashtra Ekikaran Samiti, formed in 1948 to campaign for the merger, marks 17 January as martyrs' day for activists shot by police in 1956.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>On 1 November 2014 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a> government changed the name from Belgaum to Belagavi. Kannada and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marathi">Marathi</a> are the predominant languages, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urdu">Urdu</a>, Konkani, Hindi and Telugu also spoken. The Suvarna Vidhana Soudha, an administrative building inaugurated in 2012, hosts the annual winter session of the Karnataka Legislative Assembly.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Belgaum” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The territory lay between <b>Belgaum</b> and Dharwad in the fertile black-soil country of northern Karnataka.</blockquote><a href="/state/kittur-state">Kittur State</a></li><li><blockquote>After accession to India Ramdurg was merged into Bombay State in 1948, and since 1956 its lands have been in <b>Belagavi</b> district of Karnataka.</blockquote><a href="/state/ramdurg-state">Ramdurg State</a></li></ul></section>
